**An intruder selfie app takes a photo with the front camera when someone enters a wrong PIN, pattern or password on your phone.** The main difference between the apps is what happens to that photo. CrookCatcher emails it to you with the phone's location, so you have it even if the phone is gone. Lockwatch also sends an email. WTMP, Third Eye and Hidden Eye save the photo on the phone.

The figures below are from each app's Google Play listing on November 27, 2026. Android has no intruder selfie feature of its own; here's [what Android does with failed unlock attempts](/blog/failed-unlock-attempts-android).

## The five apps compared

| | CrookCatcher | Lockwatch | WTMP | Third Eye | Hidden Eye |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Photo on wrong PIN | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Where the photo goes | Email, with GPS and address | Email, with GPS | In-app report | In-app photo log | Phone folder |
| Downloads | 10M+ | 5M+ | 10M+ | 5M+ | 5M+ |
| Rating | 4.7 | 4.4 | 4.7 | 4.4 | 3.4 |
| Last updated | Nov 2026 | Jun 2026 | Oct 2026 | Aug 2026 | Oct 2019 |

All five are free to install.

## What matters when you choose

- **Where the photo goes.** A photo that's only saved on the phone is gone when the phone is stolen. For theft, you need an alert that leaves the phone: an email with the photo.
- **Location.** A photo shows who. A location shows where the phone was at that moment, which gives the police far more to go on.
- **Whether it's still maintained.** Android regularly changes its rules for background apps and the camera. An app that hasn't been updated for years may stop working on a new phone.
- **Lock screen or app lock.** Some "intruder selfie" features are part of an app lock and only react to a wrong password for one app. The five apps here all watch the phone's lock screen.

## 1. CrookCatcher

CrookCatcher takes a photo when someone enters a wrong PIN, pattern or password, and emails it to you with the GPS location and an estimated address. All of that is in the free version. If the phone has no connection at that moment, the email goes out as soon as it reconnects.

It has been on Google Play since 2014, has more than 12 million downloads, and takes around 1.2 million intruder photos a day.

Pro adds video with sound, a photo from the back camera, Google Drive backup, an alarm, [a message on the lock screen](/blog/lock-screen-message-android), [a fake home screen](/blog/fake-home-screen-android) and a pattern lock on CrookCatcher itself, so nobody else can switch it off.

- **Good for:** a stolen phone and a phone someone tried to get into alike, because the photo and the location reach your inbox and don't stay on the phone.

