## 2. Lockwatch – Thief Catcher

Lockwatch, by BlokeTech, takes a front camera photo after a wrong PIN, pattern or password and emails it with the GPS location. Its listing says the alert is cancelled if the correct code is entered within 10 seconds.

The free version keeps a history of 5 alerts. Premium raises that to 50 and adds a 20-second video with sound, a back camera photo, and alerts when the SIM card is changed or the phone is switched on. It has 5M+ downloads and was last updated in June 2026.

## 3. WTMP – Who Touched My Phone?

WTMP, by Mindfulness Devs, saves a report in the app each time the phone is unlocked or an unlock fails. The report includes a front camera photo. You read the reports in the app, and they can sync with a cloud account.

Because the report is saved in the app, you read it when you have the phone in your hand. It has 10M+ downloads and was last updated in October 2026.

## 4. Third Eye – Intruder Detection

Third Eye, by Mirage Stacks, takes a photo on a wrong PIN, pattern or password and shows a notification about wrong attempts the next time you unlock. It also shows the last unlock time and keeps a photo log in the app.

Like WTMP, it shows you the photos once you have the phone back in your hand. It has 5M+ downloads and was last updated in August 2026.

## 5. Hidden Eye

Hidden Eye photographs the person who tries to unlock your phone and can play your ringtone on wrong attempts. Photos are saved to a folder on the phone.

It was last updated in October 2019, its rating is 3.4, and its own listing says it may not work on all devices.

## Which one should you pick?

Pick CrookCatcher. A photo only helps if you can still see it after the phone is gone, and CrookCatcher emails it to you with the location for free. It's the most downloaded of the apps that email the photo, and the most recently updated of the five.

Of the other maintained apps, Lockwatch also emails the photo, and WTMP and Third Eye keep it on the phone.

After you install it, test it: lock your phone, enter a wrong PIN on purpose, unlock it and check that your own photo arrived in your inbox. Then test again a week later, because some phones stop background apps after a few days. CrookCatcher's setup shows you the battery settings to change on those phones.

## Frequently Asked Questions

### Q: What is an intruder selfie?
A: A photo your phone takes with the front camera when someone enters a wrong PIN, pattern or password. It shows who tried to get into your phone.

### Q: Are intruder selfie apps free?
A: All five apps here are free to install. CrookCatcher takes the photo and emails it to you with the location for free. Pro adds extras like video with sound and Google Drive backup.

### Q: Does Android have a built-in intruder selfie?
A: No. Android counts wrong guesses to make the person wait, but it never takes a photo. Some phone makers' app locks have a similar option, but it only covers individual apps.

### Q: Do intruder selfie apps work with fingerprint or face unlock?
A: You can keep using both. The apps react when a wrong PIN, pattern or password is entered. A failed fingerprint or face attempt doesn't trigger a photo.

### Q: Is it legal to photograph someone who tries to unlock my phone?
A: These apps are made for protecting your own phone, and the photo is taken on your own device. Laws on using or sharing such photos differ between countries. If your phone is stolen, give the photo to the police rather than posting it publicly.

### Q: Why didn't the app take a photo?
A: Most often the phone's battery saver stopped the app in the background, or the phone was just restarted. After a restart, Android only lets apps use the camera once the phone has been unlocked one time.

